shunt (2.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Drop shunt-first-boot.service. It was a leftover of the pre-package install
    method: enabled by the postinst, but its ExecStart pointed at a source tree
    the package never creates, so on a fresh install it failed at every boot.
    Nothing depended on it -- first-time setup is done by the postinst and
    shunt-setup. Upgrading disables and removes any copy left behind by 2.0.0.
  * Ship /etc/sysctl.d/90-shunt.conf. IP forwarding, route_localnet, BBR with
    fair queueing and disabled IPv6 were previously written by hand by the old
    installer, which meant a box installed from the package alone was missing
    them. Disabling IPv6 is the load-bearing one: a live IPv6 path leaves
    through the WAN port unsplit and untunnelled. It is a conffile, so local
    edits survive upgrades; migrating retires the old xray-* drop-ins.
  * Generate /opt/shunt/.secret at install time. Session tokens are signed
    with it, and only the pre-package install.sh ever created it, so a gateway
    installed from the package alone fell back to the literal default in
    main.py -- a value published with the source, which is enough to mint a
    valid admin token for any such gateway without knowing its password. An
    existing secret is kept, and a migration carries the old one across.
  * Bound the geo rule set download. It used --connect-timeout, which only
    limits the handshake: a connection that opened and then delivered nothing
    hung forever, stalling the postinst -- and the weekly timer -- with no
    indication of what was wrong. It now aborts a transfer that stays under
    1 kB/s for a minute, which is the right test for a 92 MB file on a slow
    link, and retries three times.
  * iptables.sh withdraws its own partial work. A failure part-way through
    "up" used to stop with the TPROXY jump already attached to PREROUTING and
    nothing listening on the tproxy port, which does not degrade a gateway --
    it silently drops every packet forwarded through it. Setup is now all or
    nothing.
  * iptables.sh no longer requires the agvpn account. adguardvpn-cli is a
    Suggests, so on a freshly installed gateway the account does not exist,
    iptables rejected the owner match outright, and routing could never start
    at all. Its direct-egress exemption is skipped with a note instead.
  * Units that cannot succeed on a fresh install no longer report faults or
    retry forever. shunt-mgmt-ap and shunt-fptn-egress are skipped by a
    condition when the files they need are absent -- a rescue AP that was
    never configured and an optional backup egress are not failures -- and
    shunt.service gives up after five attempts rather than restarting every
    five seconds on a machine nobody has configured yet.
  * Add packaging/mkiso.sh, which builds an unattended Debian 13 amd64
    installer image with both packages preinstalled.

